PART 4 — REAL COMBAT DECISION-MAKING

Every attack must have a purpose.

The CPU should not merely select attacks because their cooldown is finished.

It must understand the reason for using each option.

Attack evaluation

Consider:

* Effective hitbox size.
* Horizontal and vertical reach.
* Relative positioning.
* Opponent movement and velocity.
* Own momentum.
* Attack startup.
* Hitbox activation.
* Recovery vulnerability.
* Expected damage.
* Expected launch.
* Directional Launch.
* Opponent Launch Point after the hit.
* Hitstun.
* Shield interaction.
* Projectile interaction.
* Combo potential.
* Mobility properties.
* Stage geometry.
* Distance from the Void.
* Risk of retaliation.
* Opportunity cost.

Generate viable attack candidates and evaluate their expected outcomes.

A fast low-damage attack may be preferable to a slow powerful attack if it is much more likely to connect.

A launcher may be preferable to a damage-building attack near the Void.

A movement attack may be useful even if dealing damage is not its primary immediate benefit.

Range intelligence

The CPU must know the effective ranges of every move.

Include the fighter’s hurtboxes, the move’s hitboxes, movement during startup, attack-specific motion, pulls, projectile trajectories, and the opponent’s likely position when the move becomes active.

Different attacks should have different ideal engagement distances.

The CPU should deliberately enter these distances.

Do not make a melee character constantly attack outside its reach.

Do not make a projectile character constantly close into melee when its ranged tools would be more advantageous.

Whiff awareness

If the CPU notices the opponent miss an attack, it should evaluate whether the remaining recovery offers a punish opportunity.

Punishment should depend on the actual recovery window and the CPU’s ability to reach the opponent before that window ends.

Do not grant supernatural instant punishments.

Attack safety

Before committing, estimate whether the attack will:

* Reach its target.
* Be blocked.
* Be interrupted.
* Leave the CPU exposed.
* Carry the CPU off a ledge.
* Allow a safer follow-up.
* Improve its position.
* Create a favorable trade.

Higher difficulties should increasingly prioritize favorable expected outcomes.

