PART 8 — EDGE-GUARDING, TRAPPING, AND RECOVERY

Edge-guarding

When the opponent is off-stage, the CPU should recognize the opportunity.

Evaluate:

* Opponent position.
* Opponent velocity.
* Remaining recovery resources, when observable or reasonably inferable.
* Available jumps.
* Likely recovery route.
* Available attacking options.
* Directional Launch.
* Own position.
* Risk of falling into the Void.

Possible edge-guarding actions:

* Hold a strategically advantageous ledge position.
* Intercept an opponent’s return.
* Attack an approaching airborne opponent.
* Launch them farther from safety.
* Use ranged attacks to limit recovery routes.
* Jump off-stage for an interception when the CPU can recover safely.
* Retreat back onto the stage when pursuit becomes dangerous.

Do not have the CPU recklessly leap into the Void whenever an opponent leaves the main floor.

Trapping

Recognize opportunities to constrain an opponent’s available movement.

Use projectiles, pulls, summons, techniques, attacks, and stage geometry to create pressure.

A trap should have a meaningful purpose:

* Force a jump.
* Limit movement.
* Create a punish opportunity.
* Catch a landing.
* Force a defensive reaction.
* Maintain stage control.
* Threaten a launch.
* Prevent an easy recovery.

Plan follow-ups based on the opponent’s response.

Recovery intelligence

When knocked away from the stage, survival becomes the highest priority.

The CPU must evaluate:

* Its current momentum.
* Launch stun.
* Remaining air jumps.
* Available air dash.
* Character-specific movement abilities.
* Free-fall restrictions.
* Stage height.
* Ledge position.
* Actual Void boundaries.
* Estimated time until elimination.

Develop an actionable recovery route rather than issuing one fixed movement direction.

Use the safest available sequence of jumps, steering, air dash, and eligible mobility attacks.

Understand that some movement attacks cannot start during an active launch or free fall.

Avoid wasting the last available recovery action.

Even Easy should actively attempt to recover.

Difficulty should determine recovery quality, not whether recovery is attempted.

