PART 9 — SHIELD AND DEFLECT INTELLIGENCE

Alva has different defensive behavior on the ground and in the air.

Ground: The Shield button holds a Shield.

Air: A fresh Shield press starts the Deflect attack.

These must remain distinct.

Ground Shield intelligence

The CPU must understand:

* Incoming hit timing.
* Shield effectiveness.
* Unblockable attacks.
* Perfect Shield opportunities.
* Shield blockstun.
* Energy costs.
* Opponent recovery.
* Whether shielding or moving is safer.
* When to lower its Shield.
* When a punish is possible after defending.

Avoid permanent turtling.

Avoid pointless repeated Shield presses.

Do not Shield against attacks that are unblockable.

Against unblockable attacks, consider jumping, movement, interruption, or other legal avoidance options.

Deflect intelligence

Deflect is not merely defensive.

The CPU must recognize Deflect as a genuine attack.

It can:

* Strike an airborne opponent.
* Deal damage.
* Launch an opponent.
* Turn incoming projectiles back.
* Create spacing.
* Punish an aerial approach.
* Finish an opponent under suitable conditions.

The shared Deflect strike deals 3 Launch Points at Base Launch 2.

Its Directional Launch depends on the fighter.

Jump-to-Deflect behavior

The CPU must be able to recognize an incoming projectile and, when legal and physically feasible, jump into an appropriate position to Deflect it.

Example:

An opponent fires a projectile.

The CPU estimates the projectile’s trajectory and arrival time.

It determines that jumping would place its Deflect hitbox on the projectile’s path during the Deflect’s active window.

It jumps, positions itself, and times the Deflect.

The reflected projectile may then threaten its original owner.

This must work as an intentional sequence rather than an accidental button press.

Do not Deflect projectiles that cannot be reached or that have already passed.

Do not assume Deflect provides immunity to every simultaneous attack.

Offensive Deflect

The CPU must sometimes use Deflect to attack an airborne opponent directly, even when no projectile exists.

Evaluate it alongside other aerial attacks.

Account for reach, damage, launch direction, Energy cost, recovery, and opportunity cost.

Defensive difficulty

Easy:
Occasional Shield use, imperfect timing, basic Deflect attempts.

Medium:
Reasonable defensive awareness and occasional successful projectile Deflects.

Hard:
Effective protection, deliberate perfect Shield attempts, strong aerial defense.

Brutal:
Excellent threat recognition, precise legal timing, intelligent counterplay, and offensive Deflect utilization.

Even Brutal must have a finite, nonzero reaction delay.

