PART 17 — EXECUTION AND INPUT RELIABILITY

CPU Intelligence needs an execution layer that converts decisions into real legal inputs.

It must:

* Produce the same standard input snapshot format as the player controller.
* Generate correct one-step button-pressed edges.
* Avoid accidental repeated presses.
* Handle releasing and re-pressing controls.
* Execute Dash and air dash through double taps.
* Handle jump hold duration.
* Respect attack buffering.
* Respect ground and air action differences.
* Respect hit-cancel conditions.
* Respect cooldowns.
* Handle landing transitions.
* Understand animation commitment.
* Cancel invalid action plans safely.
* Recover from failed input sequences.
* Preserve correct targeting and facing.

Do not create hidden gameplay advantages.

Preserve existing attack-facing behavior where it is part of the CPU controller contract.

A CPU should not become stuck because an attack was rejected, a target moved, an intended jump did not start, or a planned Dash was unavailable.

Provide deterministic fallback behavior that continues pursuing a meaningful objective.

